    "title": "Multiplying a Column-Vector By a Row-Vector"
  },
  "question": {
    ":question/id": "15b00e28-3515-52ed-ae90-c36185bb403d",
    ":question/problem": "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=$\n\n| {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |\n| {{field-7}} | {{field-8}} | {{field-9}} |\n| {{field-10}} | {{field-11}} | {{field-12}} |",
    ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Here, the first factor is $4\\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n4\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}✓\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 3=4\\times 3\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-5) & 1\\cdot 1 \\\\ 0\\cdot 1 & 0\\cdot (-5) & 0\\cdot 1 \\\\ 0\\cdot 1 & 0\\cdot (-5) & 0\\cdot 1 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 1 & (-1)\\cdot (-5) & (-1)\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
